{"id":"refine_20260929115058713","summary":"Saved a global ARC memory describing marker-stencil replication from task-3ca7e4c6.","rationale":"Three demonstrations and a successful test solve support treating the small third-color object as a stencil, matching its occupied positions on zero-valued cells, and stamping the stencil color at those positions.","expectedOutcome":"Future ARC solvers can recognize this transformation, identify the stencil offsets and marker color, and preserve all nonmatching cells; validate against recurring noisy-grid instances with a small colored seed.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_marker_stencil_replication_in_noisy_grids","title":"ARC marker-stencil replication in noisy grids","content":"In ARC grids with a noisy two-color field and a small connected object in a third color, consider whether the object is a stencil to replicate. Record the marker object's occupied-cell offsets and color. At each in-bounds translation where every stencil-occupied position in the original grid is background zero, recolor those positions with the marker color. Preserve all other cells, including the original marker and noisy nonzero cells; overlapping matches form a union. Evidence: three demos and a successful test solve for task-3ca7e4c6. In its test, the marker color was 9 and the offsets were {(0,0),(0,2),(1,0),(1,1)}; the replicated color was 9, not the dominant noisy-grid color 2.","metadata":{"scope":"global","source_task":"task-3ca7e4c6"},"reason":"The demos and successful solve establish a reusable ARC transformation that was initially misidentified; persisting the match condition and marker-color rule may prevent repeat errors.","after":{"id":"arc_marker_stencil_replication_in_noisy_grids","kind":"memory","title":"ARC marker-stencil replication in noisy grids","content":"In ARC grids with a noisy two-color field and a small connected object in a third color, consider whether the object is a stencil to replicate. Record the marker object's occupied-cell offsets and color. At each in-bounds translation where every stencil-occupied position in the original grid is background zero, recolor those positions with the marker color. Preserve all other cells, including the original marker and noisy nonzero cells; overlapping matches form a union. Evidence: three demos and a successful test solve for task-3ca7e4c6. In its test, the marker color was 9 and the offsets were {(0,0),(0,2),(1,0),(1,1)}; the replicated color was 9, not the dominant noisy-grid color 2.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","source_task":"task-3ca7e4c6"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:51:05.856Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:51:05.856Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682203/attempts/5aeabceef638470ab98c0ce83fca400e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929115905327","summary":"Updated the global noisy-grid stencil memory with a validated task-3ca7e4c6 example.","rationale":"The task was solved correctly by preserving the color-5 stencil and stamping it at every in-bounds translation whose stencil offsets all overlay zeros; the trajectory records the exact offsets and 14 valid placements.","expectedOutcome":"Future solvers can apply the generalized rule and use the confirmed offsets and placement count as a reference when this task recurs.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_noisy_grid_stencil_replication_place_stencil_only_on_zero_matches","title":"ARC noisy-grid stencil replication: place stencil only on zero matches","content":"In ARC grids with a noisy two-color field and a small connected object in a third color, consider whether the object is a stencil to replicate. Record its occupied-cell offsets and color, preserve the source stencil, and scan every in-bounds translation. If every stencil-occupied cell overlays the designated match color (often 0), recolor exactly those cells with the stencil color; leave all other cells unchanged. Validated on task-3ca7e4c6: a color-5 stencil in a 0/4 field had 3x3 offsets (0,2), (1,1), (1,2), (2,0), (2,2); exhaustive scanning found 14 placements where all offsets were 0, and the resulting submission was correct.","path":"arc_tasks","metadata":{"scope":"global","version":3,"validated_task":"task-3ca7e4c6"},"reason":"Adds a successfully validated recurring-task example and concrete stencil offsets to the existing general memory.","before":{"id":"arc_noisy_grid_stencil_replication_place_stencil_only_on_zero_matches","kind":"memory","title":"ARC noisy-grid stencil replication: place stencil only on zero matches","content":"Observed task-3ca7e4c6 with three demos. A small connected object in a noisy two-color field is a stencil: preserve the original object, then find translations where every stencil-occupied cell overlays the noise color 0, and recolor exactly those matching cells to the object color; preserve all other cells. In the test with color-5 stencil, offsets from its top-left bounding box were [(0,2),(1,1),(2,1),(2,2),(3,0),(3,1),(4,2),(4,3)]. Sliding this exact set over the grid and requiring every covered input cell to be 0 yielded two translated copies; replacing their occupied cells gave the correct output. Do not assume the dominant background is the overlay target. Guesses overlaying uniform background 9 were incorrect. Keep stencil pixels/orientation exact; do not fill the entire bounding rectangle.","path":"arc_tasks","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"task_id":"task-3ca7e4c6","version":2},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:52:45.165884+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:52:54.975116+00:00","version":2},"after":{"id":"arc_noisy_grid_stencil_replication_place_stencil_only_on_zero_matches","kind":"memory","title":"ARC noisy-grid stencil replication: place stencil only on zero matches","content":"In ARC grids with a noisy two-color field and a small connected object in a third color, consider whether the object is a stencil to replicate. Record its occupied-cell offsets and color, preserve the source stencil, and scan every in-bounds translation. If every stencil-occupied cell overlays the designated match color (often 0), recolor exactly those cells with the stencil color; leave all other cells unchanged. Validated on task-3ca7e4c6: a color-5 stencil in a 0/4 field had 3x3 offsets (0,2), (1,1), (1,2), (2,0), (2,2); exhaustive scanning found 14 placements where all offsets were 0, and the resulting submission was correct.","path":"arc_tasks","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","version":3,"validated_task":"task-3ca7e4c6"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:52:45.165884+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:59:10.919Z","version":3},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682203/attempts/5aeabceef638470ab98c0ce83fca400e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929120214262","summary":"Added a global ARC memory for filling one-cell horizontal gaps between same-row markers.","rationale":"The task demo and solved test both confirm that only the background cell between same-row markers two columns apart is recolored to 2; original cells and all other cells remain unchanged.","expectedOutcome":"Future solvers can recognize this gap-filling rule and validate the matching marker pair and colors before applying it.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_fill_single_cell_gaps_between_horizontal_markers_task_9a0028f5","title":"ARC: fill single-cell horizontal gaps between markers (task-9a0028f5)","content":"Observed task-9a0028f5. In a uniform-background grid, when same-row marker pixels are separated by exactly one background cell (columns c and c+2), recolor only the intervening cell to color 2. Preserve the markers, background elsewhere, and all other cells. Confirmed on test rows with color-3 markers on color-9 background; the demo showed the analogous gap fill between color-4 markers on color-8 background. Do not extend beyond the gap between the adjacent markers.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The demonstrated and correctly solved rule is a compact, reusable ARC transformation.","after":{"id":"arc_fill_single_cell_gaps_between_horizontal_markers_task_9a0028f5","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: fill single-cell horizontal gaps between markers (task-9a0028f5)","content":"Observed task-9a0028f5. In a uniform-background grid, when same-row marker pixels are separated by exactly one background cell (columns c and c+2), recolor only the intervening cell to color 2. Preserve the markers, background elsewhere, and all other cells. Confirmed on test rows with color-3 markers on color-9 background; the demo showed the analogous gap fill between color-4 markers on color-8 background. Do not extend beyond the gap between the adjacent markers.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:02:18.791Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:02:18.791Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682203/attempts/5aeabceef638470ab98c0ce83fca400e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929121300981","summary":"Update the task-18ac0783 memory with the validated rectangle bounds and the key lesson to infer exact bounds before repairing noise.","rationale":"The instance was solved after repeated incorrect submissions caused by overextending the color-5 rectangle at rows 4–10; demos confirm that isolated contaminants are removed and true rectangles are restored exactly.","expectedOutcome":"Future solvers can identify the correct rectangle extents before editing, avoid crossing blank separators, and validate by preserving each bounded object while removing off-object singleton noise.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_noisy_grid_remove_isolated_intrusions_and_repair_rectangular_objects","title":"ARC noisy grid: remove isolated intrusions and repair rectangular objects","content":"In ARC grids with a dominant uniform background and solid rectangular objects of various colors, isolated contaminant cells outside objects should be restored to the background, and contaminant pixels or holes inside true rectangles should be replaced with the rectangle's color. First catalogue each candidate rectangle's exact bounding box from its repeated solid row and column runs; do not extend across blank separators or infer bounds from nearby objects. In task-18ac0783, the validated rectangles were: color 5 at rows 4–10, columns 2–3; color 5 at rows 4–10, columns 9–12; color 9 at rows 0–2, columns 11–13; color 9 at rows 4–10, columns 6–7; color 9 at rows 12–17, columns 5–11; color 9 at rows 16–21, columns 13–15; color 9 at rows 17–20, columns 0–1; and color 5 at rows 22–23, columns 7–10. The first color-5 object is only two columns wide, not four; overextending its bounds caused repeated wrong submissions. Demos confirm the general operation: preserve and repair solid rectangles, clear isolated off-object noise.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Adds a concrete, validated bounds example and addresses the specific repeated error evidenced in this solved instance.","before":{"id":"arc_noisy_grid_remove_isolated_intrusions_and_repair_rectangular_objects","kind":"memory","title":"ARC noisy grid: remove isolated intrusions and repair rectangular objects","content":"Observed task-18ac0783 over 8 demos. Grids have a dominant uniform background and multiple solid rectangular objects in other colors. Many single-cell contaminants (various colors) occur on the background and overwrite cells inside rectangles. Output removes isolated contaminants by restoring background; when a contaminant lies within a coherent rectangular object, restore the object color to complete its rectangle. Preserve the legitimate object rectangles and background. Demos showed target objects as complete rectangles after cleanup, including restoration of overwritten cells; isolated marker pixels of colors such as 2, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9 were replaced by background. Be cautious to infer each rectangle's exact bounds from unaffected rows/cols; don't extend it just because nearby cells share a color. Test instance had many color-6 singletons and rectangles of 1, 3, 7, 9 plus a coherent 8 block. Our submissions were repeatedly wrong, probably due incorrect inferred rectangle bounds; use consistent rectangle geometry and distinguish separate neighboring objects.","path":"arc_tasks","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:57:09.730510+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:57:09.730526+00:00","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_noisy_grid_remove_isolated_intrusions_and_repair_rectangular_objects","kind":"memory","title":"ARC noisy grid: remove isolated intrusions and repair rectangular objects","content":"In ARC grids with a dominant uniform background and solid rectangular objects of various colors, isolated contaminant cells outside objects should be restored to the background, and contaminant pixels or holes inside true rectangles should be replaced with the rectangle's color. First catalogue each candidate rectangle's exact bounding box from its repeated solid row and column runs; do not extend across blank separators or infer bounds from nearby objects. In task-18ac0783, the validated rectangles were: color 5 at rows 4–10, columns 2–3; color 5 at rows 4–10, columns 9–12; color 9 at rows 0–2, columns 11–13; color 9 at rows 4–10, columns 6–7; color 9 at rows 12–17, columns 5–11; color 9 at rows 16–21, columns 13–15; color 9 at rows 17–20, columns 0–1; and color 5 at rows 22–23, columns 7–10. The first color-5 object is only two columns wide, not four; overextending its bounds caused repeated wrong submissions. Demos confirm the general operation: preserve and repair solid rectangles, clear isolated off-object noise.","path":"arc_tasks","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:57:09.730510+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:13:06.562Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682203/attempts/5aeabceef638470ab98c0ce83fca400e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929125621408","summary":"Saved the validated task-790052e7 panel-intersection rule as a global ARC memory.","rationale":"The rule was confirmed by the demonstration and produced the correct output for the test: align the two same-sized panels and mark positions where both contain foreground.","expectedOutcome":"Future solvers can recognize this panel-comparison task and produce the panel-sized intersection, preserving alignment and using 0 elsewhere.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_divider_panels_foreground_intersection_task_790052e7","title":"ARC divider-separated panels: foreground intersection (task-790052e7)","content":"For task-790052e7, a full vertical divider separates two equal-sized binary-pattern panels. The panels may use different foreground colors (test: left 8, right 6; demo: left 5, right 7), with 0 as background. Produce an output the size of one panel, preserving row and column alignment: write color 2 exactly where both panels have foreground, and 0 elsewhere. Ignore the divider. This rule was confirmed on a demo and solved test.","path":"arc_tasks","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The panel intersection rule was demonstrated and validated on the solved test, making it a reusable task-specific ARC lesson.","after":{"id":"arc_divider_panels_foreground_intersection_task_790052e7","kind":"memory","title":"ARC divider-separated panels: foreground intersection (task-790052e7)","content":"For task-790052e7, a full vertical divider separates two equal-sized binary-pattern panels. The panels may use different foreground colors (test: left 8, right 6; demo: left 5, right 7), with 0 as background. Produce an output the size of one panel, preserving row and column alignment: write color 2 exactly where both panels have foreground, and 0 elsewhere. Ignore the divider. This rule was confirmed on a demo and solved test.","path":"arc_tasks","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:56:26.964Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:56:26.964Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682203/attempts/5aeabceef638470ab98c0ce83fca400e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929130023673","summary":"Updated the existing task-6d5975a4 memory with the confirmed uniform-input result and a cautious note about the broader transformation.","rationale":"The harness already contains a memory for this task, so updating it avoids creating a duplicate; the trajectory confirms the 3H×2W size relation and the exact output for the tested 2×1 grid, while the broader rule should be presented cautiously as requested.","expectedOutcome":"Future solvers can return the confirmed 6×2 all-2 output for this input and treat the general transformation as uncertain; validate against the demonstrated size changes and known test case.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_task_6d5975a4_reverse_concatenate_rows_and_repeat","title":"ARC task-6d5975a4: 3H×2W output pattern, with confirmed uniform case","content":"For task-6d5975a4, demonstrations establish that an H×W input maps to a 3H×2W output (examples: 6×8→18×16 and 10×5→30×10). The outputs show repeated/reflected spatial structure, but treat the general transformation cautiously unless its exact rule is independently verified. Confirmed test case: input [[2],[2]] maps to a 6×2 grid filled with 2: [[2,2],[2,2],[2,2],[2,2],[2,2],[2,2]].","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Preserve the confirmed input-output case while avoiding overclaiming a general rule.","before":{"id":"arc_task_6d5975a4_reverse_concatenate_rows_and_repeat","kind":"memory","title":"ARC task-6d5975a4: reverse-concatenate rows and repeat","content":"For task-6d5975a4, output is 3x input height and 2x input width. Reverse input row order vertically and repeat each resulting row three times; horizontally concatenate each row reversed (left half) with the original row (right half). Demo 1x5 [0,6,3,4,0] becomes three rows [0,4,3,6,0,0,6,3,4,0]. The 8x6 demo confirms vertical reversal: first output row corresponds to the reversed last input row. Preserve colors. For a 2x3 all-2 test, the result is a 6x6 all-2 grid.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:56:53.667767+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:56:53.667784+00:00","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_task_6d5975a4_reverse_concatenate_rows_and_repeat","kind":"memory","title":"ARC task-6d5975a4: 3H×2W output pattern, with confirmed uniform case","content":"For task-6d5975a4, demonstrations establish that an H×W input maps to a 3H×2W output (examples: 6×8→18×16 and 10×5→30×10). The outputs show repeated/reflected spatial structure, but treat the general transformation cautiously unless its exact rule is independently verified. Confirmed test case: input [[2],[2]] maps to a 6×2 grid filled with 2: [[2,2],[2,2],[2,2],[2,2],[2,2],[2,2]].","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:56:53.667767+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:00:33.884Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682203/attempts/5aeabceef638470ab98c0ce83fca400e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929130158495","summary":"Added a global ARC memory for completing periodic stripe and grid patterns from sparse or partial demonstrations.","rationale":"The task demonstrations show repeated patterns at several different periods, including uniform stripes and a two-dimensional diagonal pattern. The test was solved by extending the six-row sequence while keeping its phase aligned to the known row indices; earlier attempts demonstrate the risk of phase errors.","expectedOutcome":"Future solvers can recognize periodic completion, infer the period from the demonstrated structure rather than assume a fixed period, and validate phase alignment against known cells.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_periodic_pattern_completion_task_8dc0657f","title":"ARC periodic stripe and grid pattern completion (task-8dc0657f)","content":"For task-8dc0657f, demonstrations show periodic completion in uniform stripes and in a two-dimensional diagonal pattern. Infer the repeating motif and its orientation from the observed structure; periods vary, so do not assume a fixed period. Fill missing zero-valued rows/cells by extending the motif, preserving the phase at the known indices. In the test, uniform rows 2–7 had values [9,1,4,6,7,6]; repeating this six-row motif with row-index alignment yields [7,6,9,1,4,6,7,6,9,1]. A prior incorrect attempt misaligned the phase near the end, so check every known stripe against its proposed motif and phase before submitting.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The demonstrations and solved test establish a reusable periodic-pattern completion rule and a concrete phase-alignment validation lesson.","after":{"id":"arc_periodic_pattern_completion_task_8dc0657f","kind":"memory","title":"ARC periodic stripe and grid pattern completion (task-8dc0657f)","content":"For task-8dc0657f, demonstrations show periodic completion in uniform stripes and in a two-dimensional diagonal pattern. Infer the repeating motif and its orientation from the observed structure; periods vary, so do not assume a fixed period. Fill missing zero-valued rows/cells by extending the motif, preserving the phase at the known indices. In the test, uniform rows 2–7 had values [9,1,4,6,7,6]; repeating this six-row motif with row-index alignment yields [7,6,9,1,4,6,7,6,9,1]. A prior incorrect attempt misaligned the phase near the end, so check every known stripe against its proposed motif and phase before submitting.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:02:06.291Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:02:06.291Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682203/attempts/5aeabceef638470ab98c0ce83fca400e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929130345574","summary":"Updated the existing global horizontal-gap memory with task-729ad7fe evidence and a grid-validation lesson.","rationale":"The demos confirm the existing rule across different backgrounds and marker colors; repeated wrong submissions came from an extra marker introduced while encoding a row, not from the rule itself.","expectedOutcome":"Future solvers should apply the established gap-fill rule while checking parsed dimensions and marker positions against the input before submission.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_fill_single_cell_gaps_between_horizontal_markers_task_9a0028f5","title":"ARC: fill single-cell horizontal gaps between markers","content":"In a uniform-background grid, when same-row marker pixels are separated by exactly one background cell (columns c and c+2), recolor only the intervening cell to color 2. Preserve markers and all other cells; do not extend filling beyond the final marker. Confirmed for task-9a0028f5 and task-729ad7fe across differing background and marker colors, including marker 8 on background 3. Before submitting, validate row lengths and compare parsed marker positions to the given input: an accidental extra marker while transcribing a row caused repeated wrong outputs despite the correct transformation rule.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Incorporates a confirmed additional task instance and an evidence-backed input-validation lesson into the matching reusable rule.","before":{"id":"arc_fill_single_cell_gaps_between_horizontal_markers_task_9a0028f5","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: fill single-cell horizontal gaps between markers (task-9a0028f5)","content":"Observed task-9a0028f5. In a uniform-background grid, when same-row marker pixels are separated by exactly one background cell (columns c and c+2), recolor only the intervening cell to color 2. Preserve the markers, background elsewhere, and all other cells. Confirmed on test rows with color-3 markers on color-9 background; the demo showed the analogous gap fill between color-4 markers on color-8 background. Do not extend beyond the gap between the adjacent markers.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:02:18.791Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:02:18.791Z","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_fill_single_cell_gaps_between_horizontal_markers_task_9a0028f5","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: fill single-cell horizontal gaps between markers","content":"In a uniform-background grid, when same-row marker pixels are separated by exactly one background cell (columns c and c+2), recolor only the intervening cell to color 2. Preserve markers and all other cells; do not extend filling beyond the final marker. Confirmed for task-9a0028f5 and task-729ad7fe across differing background and marker colors, including marker 8 on background 3. Before submitting, validate row lengths and compare parsed marker positions to the given input: an accidental extra marker while transcribing a row caused repeated wrong outputs despite the correct transformation rule.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:02:18.791Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:03:52.440Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682203/attempts/5aeabceef638470ab98c0ce83fca400e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929130541041","summary":"Added a global memory for task-c81435d6’s validated seed-pattern replication rule.","rationale":"Eight demonstrations and the correctly solved test support identifying the colored seed’s occupied-cell offsets, then copying those offsets only to translated placements where every target cell is background zero.","expectedOutcome":"Future solvers can recognize this noisy-grid stencil task, preserve the original grid and seed, and validate candidate placements against all seed offsets before recoloring.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_noisy_binary_seed_replication_task_c81435d6","title":"ARC noisy binary grids: replicate seed at zero-only matches (task-c81435d6)","content":"For task-c81435d6, a noisy binary grid contains a compact connected seed object in a distinct color. Preserve the original grid and seed. Record the seed's occupied-cell offsets, then scan translated placements; replicate the seed color at a placement only when every corresponding target cell is background zero. Leave all other cells unchanged. The rule was confirmed across eight demonstrations with varied grid sizes, backgrounds, and seed colors; it solved the test instance.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Persist the validated, recurring ARC transformation so future instances can use the exact stencil-match criterion instead of guessing at noise patterns.","after":{"id":"arc_noisy_binary_seed_replication_task_c81435d6","kind":"memory","title":"ARC noisy binary grids: replicate seed at zero-only matches (task-c81435d6)","content":"For task-c81435d6, a noisy binary grid contains a compact connected seed object in a distinct color. Preserve the original grid and seed. Record the seed's occupied-cell offsets, then scan translated placements; replicate the seed color at a placement only when every corresponding target cell is background zero. Leave all other cells unchanged. The rule was confirmed across eight demonstrations with varied grid sizes, backgrounds, and seed colors; it solved the test instance.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:05:47.345Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:05:47.345Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682203/attempts/5aeabceef638470ab98c0ce83fca400e/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929130609873","summary":"Saved the validated task-dd6a9489 tile-decoding rule as a global memory.","rationale":"The demo and correctly solved test support the same rule: divide the input into aligned 3×3 tiles and represent each tile by its signal color, ignoring noise colors 0 and 5.","expectedOutcome":"Future solvers can recognize this transformation, infer tile size from the input/output dimensions, preserve tile alignment, and validate the decoded output dimensions.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_task_dd6a9489_decode_signal_colors_from_noisy_tiles","title":"ARC task-dd6a9489: decode signal colors from noisy 3×3 tiles","content":"For task-dd6a9489, partition the input into aligned 3×3 tiles; each tile maps to one output cell, so a 24×24 input yields an 8×8 output and a 9×9 input yields a 3×3 output. Within each tile, ignore noise/background colors 0 and 5. The output cell is the non-0/non-5 signal color present in that tile, or 0 if there is none. Preserve row-major tile alignment and infer/check tile size from input and output dimensions. Validated test output for the 9×9 input: [[8,0,8],[0,8,0],[0,3,3]].","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The demo established the 3×3 tiling pattern, and the submitted output for the test was confirmed correct.","after":{"id":"arc_task_dd6a9489_decode_signal_colors_from_noisy_tiles","kind":"memory","title":"ARC task-dd6a9489: decode signal colors from noisy 3×3 tiles","content":"For task-dd6a9489, partition the input into aligned 3×3 tiles; each tile maps to one output cell, so a 24×24 input yields an 8×8 output and a 9×9 input yields a 3×3 output. Within each tile, ignore noise/background colors 0 and 5. The output cell is the non-0/non-5 signal color present in that tile, or 0 if there is none. Preserve row-major tile alignment and infer/check tile size from input and output dimensions. Validated test output for the 9×9 input: [[8,0,8],[0,8,0],[0,3,3]].","path":"general","