{"id":"refine_20260929143743308","summary":"Updated the global task-6d5975a4 memory with the confirmed one-row transformation and the solved test result.","rationale":"The current harness already contains a task-specific memory, and the trajectory provides direct evidence that a 1×N input maps to three identical rows of reverse(input) concatenated with input; the multirow demo still does not establish a precise general rule.","expectedOutcome":"On recurrence, solvers can apply the validated one-row rule and use the 3H×2W dimensions as a constraint without overgeneralizing the unresolved multirow behavior.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_task_6d5975a4_reverse_concatenate_rows_and_repeat","title":"ARC task-6d5975a4: 3H×2W output, confirmed one-row reflection rule","content":"For task-6d5975a4, an H×W input maps to a 3H×2W output. Confirmed for 1×N inputs: output consists of three identical rows, each formed by reverse(input row) concatenated with the original input row. This rule correctly solved the 1×12 test [5,0,1,3,4,3,7,6,9,3,2,8], producing three copies of [8,2,3,9,6,7,3,4,3,1,0,5,5,0,1,3,4,3,7,6,9,3,2,8]. A 1×10 demo confirms the same rule. A 2×13 demo confirms output dimensions 6×26 but has a nontrivial pattern; do not assume the one-row rule directly generalizes to multirow inputs. Infer multirow behavior from demonstrations before submitting.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Preserves the validated one-row rule and successful test while clearly limiting the unresolved multirow generalization.","before":{"id":"arc_task_6d5975a4_reverse_concatenate_rows_and_repeat","kind":"memory","title":"ARC task-6d5975a4: 3H×2W output pattern, with confirmed uniform case","content":"For task-6d5975a4, demonstrations establish that an H×W input maps to a 3H×2W output (examples: 6×8→18×16 and 10×5→30×10). The outputs show repeated/reflected spatial structure, but treat the general transformation cautiously unless its exact rule is indepen